# Abstract

This work explores the possibility that the well-known Koide relation is not fundamentally a mass relation but instead reflects a deeper organizational property of three-state fermion families.

The central hypothesis is that fermion families can be arranged along an axis spanning:

- Identity (unity, coherence)
- Differentiation (speciation, distinction)

Within this framework:

- Neutrinos occupy the connectivity/coherence pole.
- Up-type quarks occupy the specialization/differentiation pole.
- Charged leptons occupy an equilibrium point between the two.

The charged-lepton Koide value,

\[
Q \approx \frac23
\]

is interpreted as a balanced organizational state rather than merely a numerical mass coincidence. The framework is explored through fermion mass relations, flavor mixing, decay complexity, accessibility, and generation identity.

---

# 1. Motivation

The charged-lepton Koide relation is

\[
Q =
\frac{
m_e+m_\mu+m_\tau
}{
\left(
\sqrt{m_e}
+\sqrt{m_\mu}
+\sqrt{m_\tau}
\right)^2
}
\]

Experimentally,

\[
Q_\ell \approx \frac23
\]

with remarkable precision. The geometric interpretation discovered by Robert Foot corresponds to a 45° angle between the charged-lepton root-mass vector and the symmetric vector (1,1,1). 【1-d97a9c】

Traditionally this has been viewed as a mass relation.

This work investigates an alternative interpretation:

> Q may measure organizational structure rather than mass hierarchy.

---

# 2. Identity and Differentiation

Consider a three-state family represented by

\[
a=(a_1,a_2,a_3)
\]

Decompose into:

\[
a=s+o
\]

where

\[
s
\]

is the common identity component and

\[
o
\]

is the differentiating component.

This naturally produces the decomposition

\[
\mathbb{R}^3
=
\mathbb{R}^1
\oplus
\mathbb{R}^2
\]

representing:

- one identity mode
- two differentiation modes

The dimensional fraction becomes

\[
\frac23
\]

suggesting a possible geometric interpretation of the Koide value.

